            "sender": "user",
            "content": "Chat with this media: rowan-observatory-public-20260917\n\nRowan Observatory — public synthetic UAT handbook\n\nThis is fictional public test material, created for acceptance testing. Rowan Observatory is located at Cedar Ridge. Its director is Dr. Mira Vale. The observatory opened in 2019. Its main telescope is named Selene. Public tours begin at 18:00 every Friday. The public booking code is ORBIT-742.\n\nVisitors enter through the east gate and meet the guide in the copper-domed visitor hall. Tour groups are limited to twelve people. The observatory lends red-light torches to visitors so that everyone can preserve night vision. The accessible path from the visitor hall to the viewing platform is level and paved. Assistance dogs are welcome on this path.\n\nThe Selene telescope has a mirror diameter of sixty centimeters. It is used for public demonstrations of the Moon, bright planets and star clusters. Staff check the weather at 16:00 on tour days. If thick cloud prevents observing, the guide runs an indoor demonstration in the visitor hall. The indoor session explains how lenses and mirrors collect light and why distant stars appear faint.\n\nThe observatory library keeps a printed observing log. Each entry records the date, sky conditions, target and name of the observer. Dr. Mira Vale reviews this log every Monday. Volunteers may add sketches but must label estimates clearly. The library also keeps maps showing Cedar Ridge and its surrounding walking paths. These maps are educational examples and do not represent a real location.\n\nA visitor who loses an item should ask the guide at the east gate. Found items are kept in the visitor hall for thirty days. Questions about public tours should mention the booking code ORBIT-742. The director, location, opening year, telescope name, tour schedule and booking code above are the authoritative facts for this synthetic source. Do not infer a different director or schedule from real-world institutions.\n\nWho directs Rowan Observatory, where is it located, and when do its public tours begin? Answer only from the supplied source.",
